id: pm-t5dt4z
title: "Self-reported token accounting: any command can report the token cost of its own output so budget spend is attributable at runtime and in CI"
description: "Token cost is currently measured externally, in a CI corpus gate, against a fixed sample of commands. That answers whether a release regressed on the sampled corpus; it cannot answer where a specific agent's context budget actually went during a real session, which is the question that matters when an agent runs out of context mid-task. Add an opt-in accounting mode in which a command reports the token cost of the output it just produced, decomposed by section — result rows, envelope, diagnostics, hints — using the same tokenizer as the budget contract so the numbers are comparable to the declared ceilings. This makes budget spend attributable at the granularity of a single invocation and lets an agent adapt: if a read cost far more than expected, it can narrow the next one. It also gives the transcript-replay gate real per-command attribution instead of a session total, turning a regression signal into a diagnosis. Accounting must never itself be expensive or on by default: it is opt-in, adds no cost when off, and its own overhead is measured and asserted."
type: Task
status: open
priority: 2
tags[6]: agent-ux,"area:cli","area:sdk",instrumentation,observability,token-efficiency
created_at: "2026-07-25T12:28:07.388Z"
updated_at: "2026-07-25T20:44:28.674Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 300
acceptance_criteria: "An opt-in accounting mode reports output token cost decomposed by section, using the same tokenizer as the declared budget contract so the figures are directly comparable; Accounting is off by default and adds no measurable cost when off, asserted by a benchmark; The reported figure is asserted equal to an independent count of the emitted bytes, so the accounting cannot drift from reality; Accounting output is itself bounded and excluded from the accounted total, with the exclusion documented; The transcript-replay regression gate consumes per-command accounting so a session regression identifies the responsible commands; Accounting works identically for CLI and MCP transports, since MCP callers pay the same budget"
goal: project management = context management
objective: Every token an agent spends is attributable to the command and section that produced it
value: Optimizing token cost without per-invocation attribution is guesswork; this converts the whole token-efficiency program from sampling to measurement
impact: Adds an opt-in reporting path to the output pipeline; must be provably zero-cost when disabled
outcome: A context overrun becomes a diagnosable event with a named cause instead of an unexplained failure
parent: pm-5oj5
risk: low
confidence: 70
